// ============================================================
// App Design - App Architecture & Data Modeling round
//
// A tab inside Roadmap -> System Design: the 45-minute round on
// classes, API endpoints and database tables. The block at the
// bottom slots it in right after Patterns & Case Studies.
// ============================================================

const APPDESIGN_TAB = {
  id: 'appdesign', t: 'App Design',
  topics: [
    {
      t: 'What this round actually is',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p"><b>45 minutes is the common length for this round</b> at most companies that run it &mdash; long enough to cover three areas at different depths, short enough that you have to prioritise out loud. It sits between the <b>System Design</b> round (boxes and arrows: load balancers, caches, queues) and the <b>Coding</b> round (write a working function).</p><div class="learn-warn"><b>The distinction that trips people up.</b> System Design asks "what <i>machines</i> do you need and how do they talk?" App Architecture &amp; Data Modeling asks "inside one service, what <i>classes</i>, <i>endpoints</i> and <i>tables</i> do you need?"<br><br>If you start drawing load balancers in this round you are in the wrong round. If you start designing class hierarchies in the System Design round, likewise.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The three areas, and the trap in the brief</div><p class="learn-p">The brief for this round is usually phrased as three things you will be assessed on:</p><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Area</th><th>What they said they want</th><th>What that means concretely</th></tr><tr><td><b>API design</b></td><td>"The top-level interface of your application with a focus more on the input and output than the underlying details"</td><td>Endpoints, request and response shapes, status codes, errors, pagination. Not implementation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Class design</b></td><td>"Each class serves a distinct purpose and demonstrates encapsulation and proper use of inheritance and composition"</td><td>This sentence is a direct request for design patterns and OO principles. Covered from <b>The nine design principles</b> onward.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Data schema</b></td><td>"Tables, columns, sensible relationships between tables, and primary and foreign keys"</td><td>An ER model, normalised, with the keys and indexes named explicitly.</td></tr></table><p class="learn-p">And then the crucial line that usually accompanies it: <i>"in the allotted 45 minutes, you will only have time to do a \'deep dive\' into two out of three main areas... That being said, you should still have sufficient time to touch on each area."</i></p><div class="learn-tip"><b>Read that as an instruction, because it is one</b><br>They are telling you the winning strategy outright: <b>touch all three, go deep on two.</b><br><br>The failure mode they are warning you about is spending 40 minutes lovingly designing class hierarchies and never drawing a single table. That reads as an incomplete answer even if the classes were brilliant. Budget your time deliberately and <b>say your budget out loud</b> at the start &mdash; it turns time management from something you might fail at into something you visibly control.</div><div class="learn-tip"><b>What is already in this app</b><br><b>Roadmap &rarr; System Design</b> has an <b>LLD Basics</b> tab (OOP deep dive, composition vs inheritance, SRP &amp; OCP, LSP/ISP/DIP) and a <b>Patterns &amp; Case Studies</b> tab (Singleton, Factory, Observer, Strategy, Decorator, Adapter, Builder, Command, State, Proxy, Chain of Responsibility) plus case studies for Parking Lot, BookMyShow, Splitwise and Elevator.<br><br>Use that for the SOLID principles and for a second take on the patterns. <b>This guide adds what that material does not have:</b> the specific shape of this 45-minute round, C++ implementations rather than pseudocode or Java, API design, real schema design with keys and indexes, and the concurrency answers. Where a case study overlaps (Parking Lot, Elevator) treat this version as the one tuned to this round\'s rubric.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Which two should you go deep on?</div><p class="learn-p">Let the problem decide, and say why you chose:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Lots of entities and relationships</b> (an e-commerce catalogue, a booking system) &rarr; go deep on <b>data schema</b> plus <b>API</b>.</li><li><b>Lots of varying behaviour</b> (a pricing engine, a notification system, a game) &rarr; go deep on <b>class design</b> plus <b>API</b>.</li><li><b>API is almost always one of the two</b>, because it is the contract that forces the other two to be concrete.</li></ul><p class="learn-p">Announce it: "This problem is relationship-heavy, so I\'d like to go deep on the schema and the API, and sketch the class design more briefly &mdash; does that work for you?" You have now demonstrated prioritisation <i>and</i> got their buy-in, which means they cannot mark you down for the area you skimmed.</p></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'The 45-minute method',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">Have one repeatable script so you are never improvising structure under pressure. This is the one I would use.</p><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Phase</th><th>What you produce</th></tr><tr><td><b>0&ndash;6</b></td><td>Requirements &amp; scope</td><td>A written list of functional requirements, the ones you are explicitly excluding, and the scale assumptions</td></tr><tr><td><b>6&ndash;10</b></td><td>Entities</td><td>A list of nouns &mdash; the core objects in the domain &mdash; and how they relate</td></tr><tr><td><b>10&ndash;22</b></td><td>Deep dive 1 (usually schema)</td><td>Tables with columns, types, primary and foreign keys, indexes</td></tr><tr><td><b>22&ndash;34</b></td><td>Deep dive 2 (usually API)</td><td>Endpoints with methods, paths, request/response bodies, status codes</td></tr><tr><td><b>34&ndash;40</b></td><td>Third area, lighter</td><td>Class sketch with the one or two patterns that genuinely fit</td></tr><tr><td><b>40&ndash;45</b></td><td>Trade-offs &amp; questions</td><td>What you would change at 100&times; scale; what you deliberately left out; your questions</td></tr></table></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Phase 1: Requirements &mdash; the six questions</div><p class="learn-p">Never start designing immediately. Their rubric explicitly rewards "requirements gathering" and "handling ambiguity". Ask these:</p><ol class="learn-list"><li><b>Who are the users, and what are the top three things they do?</b> This gives you your entities and your endpoints in one answer.</li><li><b>What is explicitly out of scope?</b> Get permission to ignore things. "Should I handle payments, or assume a payment service exists?"</li><li><b>Roughly what scale?</b> Hundreds of users or hundreds of millions? This decides whether you normalise hard or denormalise for reads.</li><li><b>Read-heavy or write-heavy?</b> Drives indexing and caching decisions.</li><li><b>What must be consistent, and what can be slightly stale?</b> The single most useful question in the whole round.</li><li><b>Is there anything unusual about this domain I should know?</b> Free information, and it signals humility.</li></ol><div class="learn-tip"><b>Then write the requirements down and get agreement</b><br>"So: users can search listings, book a slot, and cancel. Out of scope: payments, notifications, admin tools. Roughly 100k users, read-heavy, and a double-booking is unacceptable so booking must be strongly consistent. Have I got that right?"<br><br>That single paragraph hits requirements gathering, scope control, scale, and consistency &mdash; four rubric items in twenty seconds. It also protects you: anything you skip later, you skipped <i>with their agreement</i>.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Phase 2: Find the nouns</div><p class="learn-p">The reliable trick for going from a vague prompt to a concrete model: <b>underline the nouns in the requirements.</b> Nouns become entities, entities become tables and classes. Verbs become API endpoints and methods.</p><p class="learn-p">"A <u>user</u> can <u>search</u> <u>listings</u> and <u>book</u> a <u>slot</u>" gives you User, Listing, Slot, Booking as entities, and search / book as operations. Then ask of each pair: one-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many? Every many-to-many becomes a join table &mdash; and spotting those early is most of data modelling.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Narrate constantly</div><p class="learn-p">Their rubric lists communication in the coding round, and this round is no different. Two habits that carry a session:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>State the alternative you rejected.</b> "I\'ll put the address in its own table rather than inline on the user, because a user can have several and we\'ll want to query by city. If addresses were single and never queried, inline would be simpler." That one sentence shows you did not just pick the first idea.</li><li><b>Flag your assumptions as assumptions.</b> "I\'m assuming a listing belongs to exactly one owner &mdash; tell me if it can be co-owned, because that changes this to a many-to-many."</li></ul></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Turning a vague prompt into a design',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">A worked example of the first ten minutes, because this is where most candidates lose the session before they have written anything.</p><div class="learn-tip"><b>Prompt:</b> "Design a system for a library."</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">What a weak candidate does</div><p class="learn-p">Immediately writes <code>class Book { string title; string author; }</code> and starts adding fields. Twenty minutes later there is a class diagram, no API, no tables, and no one has established whether this library lends e-books, charges fines, or has multiple branches.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">What you do instead</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Step 1 &mdash; ask the six questions.</b> Suppose the answers come back: physical books only, multiple branches, members can borrow and reserve, fines for late returns, about 50,000 members, read-heavy, and a book must never be lent to two people at once.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Step 2 &mdash; write the scope out loud.</b></p><div class="learn-code">IN SCOPE                          OUT OF SCOPE\n- search the catalogue            - payments / fine collection\n- borrow and return               - e-books, audiobooks\n- reserve (hold) a title          - member registration flow\n- fines accrue on late return     - staff / admin tooling\n- multiple branches\n\nSCALE: ~50k members, ~200k copies, read-heavy\nCONSISTENCY: a copy may never be double-lent -> needs a transaction</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Step 3 &mdash; the nouns, and the one distinction that matters.</b></p><div class="learn-tip">Here is the modelling insight this problem is really testing, and it generalises far beyond libraries: <b>a Book is not a Copy.</b><br><br>"The Hobbit" is a <i>title</i> &mdash; it has an author, an ISBN, a description. But you do not borrow a title, you borrow a specific physical <i>copy</i> sitting on a specific branch\'s shelf, with its own condition and barcode. One title, many copies.<br><br>Miss this and every downstream design is wrong: you cannot express "we have 3 copies, 2 are out", you cannot track which branch holds what, and you cannot lend the same title to two people correctly. <b>Spotting the abstract-versus-physical split is the single most valuable modelling habit</b>, and it recurs constantly: Product vs. Inventory Item, Flight vs. Seat, Movie vs. Screening, Recipe vs. Batch.</div><div class="learn-code">Member    Title (the work)    Copy (a physical object)    Branch\nLoan      Reservation         Fine\n\nTitle  1 --- * Copy          a title has many copies\nBranch 1 --- * Copy          each copy lives at one branch\nMember 1 --- * Loan          a member has many loans over time\nCopy   1 --- * Loan          a copy has many loans over time\n   => Loan is the join between Member and Copy, plus dates</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Step 4 &mdash; state your plan and time budget.</b> "This is entity-heavy, so I\'d like to spend most time on the schema and API, and sketch classes at the end. Starting with the schema."</p><p class="learn-p">You are now ten minutes in with a scoped problem, an agreed entity model, a stated plan, and you have already demonstrated four rubric items. Everything after this is filling in detail.</p></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'API design',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">The brief for this area: "the top-level interface of your application with a focus more on the input and output than the underlying details." So they want the <b>contract</b>, not the implementation. Almost always one of your two deep dives, because it forces everything else to be concrete.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The shape of a REST API</div><p class="learn-p">The core idea: your API exposes <b>resources</b> (nouns), and HTTP methods are the <b>verbs</b> that act on them. Get the nouns right and the endpoints write themselves.</p><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Method</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Safe?</th><th>Idempotent?</th></tr><tr><td>GET</td><td>Read; never changes anything</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>POST</td><td>Create a new item, or trigger an action</td><td>No</td><td><b>No</b></td></tr><tr><td>PUT</td><td>Replace an item entirely</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>PATCH</td><td>Update part of an item</td><td>No</td><td>Usually</td></tr><tr><td>DELETE</td><td>Remove an item</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></table><p class="learn-p"><b>Idempotent</b> &mdash; doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once.</p><p class="learn-p">DELETE is idempotent: delete booking 7, then delete it again &mdash; it is still just gone. POST is not: post a booking twice and you have two bookings. This matters because networks fail. If a client sends a request and the connection drops, it does not know whether the server processed it. For an idempotent method it can just retry. For POST it cannot, without help.</p><div class="learn-tip"><b>The idempotency answer that impresses</b><br>When you design a "create" or "pay" endpoint, say this:<br><br>"POST isn\'t idempotent, so a client that times out can\'t safely retry &mdash; it might double-book. I\'d have the client generate an <b>idempotency key</b> (a UUID) and send it as a header. The server stores the key with the result; if the same key arrives again it returns the original response instead of creating a second booking."<br><br>This is exactly the kind of "beyond the requirements guardrail" their System Design rubric asks for, and it applies here too. Very few candidates raise it unprompted.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Designing the endpoints</div><p class="learn-p">Rules that keep you out of trouble:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Plural nouns for collections</b>: <code>/books</code>, not <code>/getBook</code>. The verb is the HTTP method, so putting a verb in the path is redundant.</li><li><b>Nest only to show ownership</b>: <code>/members/42/loans</code> is good when loans only exist within a member. Do not nest more than two levels deep &mdash; past that, use query parameters.</li><li><b>Filtering, sorting and paging are query parameters</b>, not paths: <code>/books?author=tolkien&amp;sort=-published&amp;limit=20</code>.</li><li><b>Actions that are not CRUD get a sub-resource</b>: returning a book is not "update loan" in any natural sense, so <code>POST /loans/{id}/return</code> is clearer and honest.</li></ul><div class="learn-code">GET    /books?q=hobbit&amp;branch=3&amp;limit=20&amp;cursor=eyJpZCI6MTIzfQ\nGET    /books/{bookId}\nGET    /books/{bookId}/copies              # which copies exist, and where\nPOST   /loans                              # borrow: body { copyId, memberId }\nPOST   /loans/{loanId}/return              # an action, not a CRUD update\nGET    /members/{memberId}/loans?status=active\nPOST   /reservations                       # body { titleId, memberId }\nDELETE /reservations/{reservationId}</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Status codes worth knowing cold</div><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Code</th><th>Name</th><th>Use it when</th></tr><tr><td>200</td><td>OK</td><td>Successful GET, or an update returning the new state</td></tr><tr><td>201</td><td>Created</td><td>POST created something. Return a <code>Location</code> header</td></tr><tr><td>202</td><td>Accepted</td><td>You queued the work; it isn\'t done yet</td></tr><tr><td>204</td><td>No Content</td><td>Success, nothing to return (a DELETE)</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Bad Request</td><td>Malformed input &mdash; the client\'s fault, syntactically</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized</td><td>We don\'t know who you are (misnamed &mdash; it means unauthenticated)</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden</td><td>We know who you are; you may not do this</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Not Found</td><td>No such resource</td></tr><tr><td>409</td><td>Conflict</td><td><b>The one to remember.</b> The request is valid but conflicts with current state &mdash; the copy is already on loan</td></tr><tr><td>422</td><td>Unprocessable</td><td>Syntactically fine, semantically invalid (return date before borrow date)</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Too Many Requests</td><td>Rate limited</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>Server Error</td><td>We broke. Never leak a stack trace</td></tr></table><p class="learn-p"><b>401 vs 403</b> and <b>400 vs 409</b> are the two distinctions interviewers actually probe. Knowing 409 for "already borrowed" instead of blanket-400 is a small detail that reads as experience.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Request and response bodies</div><p class="learn-p">Show the actual JSON. It costs thirty seconds and makes the contract unambiguous.</p><div class="learn-code">POST /loans\nIdempotency-Key: 9f1c...\n\n{ "copyId": "cp_8891", "memberId": "mb_42" }\n\n201 Created\nLocation: /loans/ln_5567\n{\n  "id":        "ln_5567",\n  "copyId":    "cp_8891",\n  "memberId":  "mb_42",\n  "borrowedAt":"2026-08-15T10:04:00Z",\n  "dueAt":     "2026-08-29T23:59:59Z",\n  "status":    "ACTIVE"\n}\n\n409 Conflict\n{\n  "error": {\n    "code":    "COPY_ALREADY_ON_LOAN",\n    "message": "Copy cp_8891 is currently on loan until 2026-08-22.",\n    "details": { "copyId": "cp_8891", "availableAt": "2026-08-22" }\n  }\n}</div><p class="learn-p">Two things to point out about that error shape: a <b>machine-readable code</b> so clients can branch on it without string-matching the message, and <b>structured details</b> so the client can show something useful. Say "I\'d use a consistent error envelope across every endpoint" &mdash; consistency is itself a design decision.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Pagination &mdash; offset vs cursor</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Offset pagination</b> &mdash; <code>?limit=20&amp;offset=40</code> &mdash; "skip 40, give me 20".</p><p class="learn-p">Simple, allows jumping to page 5. Two real problems: the database must count through all skipped rows, so deep pages get slow; and if an item is inserted while the user pages, rows shift and they see a duplicate or miss one.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Cursor (keyset) pagination</b> &mdash; <code>?limit=20&amp;cursor=abc</code> &mdash; "give me 20 after this exact item".</p><p class="learn-p">The cursor encodes the sort key of the last row seen, so the query becomes <code>WHERE (created_at, id) &lt; (...) ORDER BY ... LIMIT 20</code>, which uses the index and stays fast at any depth. Cannot jump to an arbitrary page. This is what large APIs use.</p><p class="learn-p">Saying "offset for an admin table where they want page numbers, cursor for an infinite feed" shows you pick by use case rather than by habit.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Versioning</div><p class="learn-p">Have an opinion, briefly: <code>/v1/books</code> in the path is the most common and the easiest to reason about; a header (<code>Accept: application/vnd.api+json;version=1</code>) is purer REST but harder to test by hand. The important part is the principle: <b>additive changes don\'t need a new version; removing or renaming a field does.</b></p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The rest of the checklist</div><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Authentication</b> &mdash; a bearer token (JWT) in the <code>Authorization</code> header; mention that the API validates it and derives the caller\'s identity rather than trusting a <code>memberId</code> in the body. That last point is a genuine security instinct: never let the client tell you who it is.</li><li><b>Rate limiting</b> &mdash; per API key, returning 429 with a <code>Retry-After</code>.</li><li><b>Long operations</b> &mdash; return 202 with a job id and let the client poll <code>/jobs/{id}</code>, rather than holding a request open.</li><li><b>Bulk endpoints</b> &mdash; if a client would loop over 1,000 calls, give them one <code>POST /loans/bulk</code>. Spotting the N+1 call pattern is a nice touch.</li></ul></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Class design in C++',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">About the book, before you start</div><p class="learn-p"><i>Head First Design Patterns</i> (Freeman &amp; Robson, O\'Reilly) is the source for the patterns in this guide, and it is <b>a genuinely good book for system design and low-level design</b> &mdash; it teaches you <i>why</i> each pattern exists and what breaks without it, instead of handing you a catalogue of UML diagrams to memorise. Worth reading end to end.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The one catch: every example in it is written in Java.</b> Java and C++ differ in exactly the places OOP interviews probe, so a pattern transliterated straight from the book will read as Java-with-different-syntax. This section is the translation layer &mdash; the OOP concepts compared between Java and C++ &mdash; and every pattern in the following topics is written in modern C++ rather than converted Java. Read this before the patterns.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Java vs C++ at a glance</div><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Java (as in the book)</th><th>C++ (what you write here)</th></tr><tr><td>Interface</td><td><code>interface Duck { void quack(); }</code></td><td>Abstract base class, all methods <b>pure virtual</b>, plus a virtual destructor</td></tr><tr><td>Object lifetime</td><td>Garbage collector reclaims it eventually</td><td>RAII &mdash; destructors run deterministically; <code>unique_ptr</code>/<code>shared_ptr</code></td></tr><tr><td>Variables</td><td>Every object is a reference; <code>Duck d</code> is a handle</td><td><code>Duck d</code> is a <b>value</b>; polymorphism needs a pointer or reference, or you slice</td></tr><tr><td>Base destructor</td><td>Not a concern</td><td><code>virtual ~Base() = default;</code> is <b>mandatory</b> for any polymorphic base</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple inheritance</td><td>Only of interfaces</td><td>Allowed of classes, with the diamond problem and <code>virtual</code> inheritance</td></tr><tr><td>Overriding</td><td><code>@Override</code> annotation, optional</td><td><code>override</code> keyword &mdash; use it always; <code>final</code> to stop the chain</td></tr><tr><td>Generics</td><td>Type-erased generics, runtime</td><td>Templates, compile-time &mdash; enables static polymorphism (CRTP) with no vtable cost</td></tr><tr><td>Passing objects</td><td>Always by reference, implicitly</td><td>Choose: by value (copies), <code>const&amp;</code> (cheap read), or by pointer (nullable)</td></tr></table><p class="learn-p">The rest of this section works through the ones that change how you actually write the pattern.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">1. C++ has no <code>interface</code> keyword</div><p class="learn-p">Java\'s <code>interface Duck { void quack(); }</code> becomes an abstract base class with <b>pure virtual</b> functions:</p><div class="learn-code">class QuackBehavior {\npublic:\n    virtual ~QuackBehavior() = default;   // &lt;-- REQUIRED. see below\n    virtual void quack() const = 0;       // "= 0" makes it pure virtual\n};\n\nclass LoudQuack : public QuackBehavior {\npublic:\n    void quack() const override { std::cout &lt;&lt; "QUACK!\\n"; }\n};</div><div class="learn-warn"><b>The virtual destructor rule &mdash; they will check this</b><br>If you delete a derived object through a base pointer and the base destructor is <b>not</b> virtual, the behaviour is undefined &mdash; in practice the derived part never gets destroyed and you leak.<div class="learn-code">Base* p = new Derived();\ndelete p;          // Base::~Base not virtual -> Derived::~Derived never runs</div><b>Rule:</b> any class you intend to inherit from polymorphically gets a <code>virtual ~Class() = default;</code>. Write it every time, unprompted. In an interview it takes two seconds and it is the single most reliable signal that you actually write C++.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">2. Ownership replaces garbage collection</div><p class="learn-p">Java hands you a reference and the collector cleans up. In C++ <b>you must say who owns what</b>, and that choice <i>is</i> part of the design.</p><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use for</th></tr><tr><td><code>std::unique_ptr&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>Exactly one owner; freed when it goes away</td><td><b>The default.</b> A Decorator wrapping its component, a Strategy held by a Context</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::shared_ptr&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>Shared ownership, reference counted</td><td>When lifetime genuinely is shared &mdash; several observers holding a subject</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::weak_ptr&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>Observes without owning; can detect death</td><td><b>Observer lists</b>, and breaking reference cycles</td></tr><tr><td><code>T&amp;</code> or <code>T*</code></td><td>Non-owning reference</td><td>A parameter you only read; a back-pointer to a parent</td></tr></table><p class="learn-p"><b>The interview line:</b> "I\'ll hold this by <code>unique_ptr</code> because ownership is exclusive; if I needed shared ownership I\'d use <code>shared_ptr</code>, but I\'d reach for that deliberately rather than by default." Reaching for <code>shared_ptr</code> everywhere is the classic sign of someone writing Java in C++.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">3. Rule of Zero, Three, Five</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Rule of Zero</b> &mdash; if your class holds its resources in types that already manage themselves (<code>std::string</code>, <code>std::vector</code>, smart pointers), write <b>no</b> destructor, copy constructor or assignment operator. The compiler\'s defaults are correct.</p><p class="learn-p">This is what you want almost always. Say "Rule of Zero" and move on.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Rule of Five</b> &mdash; if you <i>do</i> write one of {destructor, copy constructor, copy assignment, move constructor, move assignment}, you probably need to consider all five.</p><p class="learn-p">Comes up when you hand-manage a resource. In a design round you should usually be avoiding that.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">4. Copies are real, and they cost</div><p class="learn-p">Java passes object references. C++ copies by default. So <code>void f(std::string s)</code> copies the whole string; <code>void f(const std::string&amp; s)</code> does not. Get this wrong in an interview and it looks like you learned C++ from Java. Defaults to use:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Read-only parameter</b> &rarr; <code>const T&amp;</code></li><li><b>The function will keep a copy</b> &rarr; take by value and <code>std::move</code> it in</li><li><b>Cheap types</b> (<code>int</code>, <code>double</code>, an enum) &rarr; by value</li></ul></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">5. <code>std::function</code> often replaces a whole class hierarchy</div><p class="learn-p">A genuinely idiomatic C++ move that the book cannot show you. If a Strategy has one method and no state, you do not need an inheritance hierarchy at all:</p><div class="learn-code">// The book\'s way: an interface plus a class per behaviour.\n// The C++ way, when the behaviour is a single function:\nclass Duck {\n    std::function&lt;void()&gt; quack_;\npublic:\n    explicit Duck(std::function&lt;void()&gt; q) : quack_(std::move(q)) {}\n    void performQuack() const { quack_(); }\n};\n\nDuck loud([]{ std::cout &lt;&lt; "QUACK!\\n"; });\nDuck mute([]{ /* nothing */ });</div><p class="learn-p"><b>When to prefer the class hierarchy anyway:</b> the behaviour has state, several related methods, or you want to name and test the behaviours independently. Being able to argue both sides is the point &mdash; their rubric explicitly rewards discussing technical trade-offs.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">6. Small keywords that signal care</div><ul class="learn-list"><li><code>override</code> &mdash; on every function overriding a virtual. The compiler then catches a typo\'d signature that would otherwise silently create a new function.</li><li><code>final</code> &mdash; on classes not designed for further inheritance.</li><li><code>explicit</code> &mdash; on single-argument constructors, to stop surprise implicit conversions.</li><li><code>enum class Status { Active, Returned };</code> &mdash; scoped and type-safe, unlike a plain <code>enum</code>.</li><li><code>const</code> on member functions that do not mutate. Const-correctness is free credibility.</li><li><code>[[nodiscard]]</code> on functions whose return value must not be ignored.</li></ul></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">7. Composition in C++</div><div class="learn-code">// Inheritance: Manager IS-A Employee\nclass Manager : public Employee { ... };\n\n// Composition: Order HAS-A payment method\nclass Order {\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;PaymentMethod&gt; payment_;   // swappable at runtime\n    std::vector&lt;LineItem&gt;          items_;     // owns its items by value\n};</div><p class="learn-p">The test to say out loud: <b>"is-a" means inheritance, "has-a" means composition</b> &mdash; and if you are ever unsure, prefer composition, because it can be changed at runtime and does not couple you to a base class\'s internals.</p></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'The nine design principles',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">These are the principles <i>Head First Design Patterns</i> builds everything on, quoted as the book states them. The patterns are just recurring applications of these. If you remember nothing else, remember the first three &mdash; they generate most good designs on their own.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">1. Identify the aspects of your application that vary and separate them from what stays the same.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> find the part that keeps changing, and pull it out into its own thing.</p><p class="learn-p">The book\'s master principle; every pattern is an instance of it. In the interview, the question to ask yourself about any design is <b>"what is most likely to change here?"</b> Then make that the part behind an interface. New payment method, new discount rule, new export format &mdash; these are the axes of change, and they should each be one new class rather than an edit to five.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">2. Program to an interface, not an implementation.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> depend on <i>what</i> something does, not on <i>which</i> concrete class does it.</p><div class="learn-code">// Bad: bound to one concrete class forever\nMySQLBookRepo repo_;\n\n// Good: any implementation will do - including a fake one in tests\nstd::unique_ptr&lt;BookRepository&gt; repo_;</div><p class="learn-p">The immediate practical payoff to mention: <b>testability</b>. If your service depends on an interface you can inject an in-memory fake and test it without a database. Interviewers love a candidate who links a design principle to testing.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">3. Favor composition over inheritance.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> build behaviour by holding other objects, rather than by inheriting from a base class.</p><p class="learn-p">Why the book pushes this so hard: inheritance is fixed at compile time and applies to all instances of the class, while composition can be swapped at runtime. Inheritance also creates the tightest possible coupling &mdash; a change to the base class ripples into every subclass. And deep hierarchies produce the class explosion the book opens with (a subclass for every combination of features).</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">4. Strive for loosely coupled designs between objects that interact.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> objects should know as little about each other as possible.</p><p class="learn-p">Observer is the poster child: the subject knows only that observers implement an interface. It does not know their concrete types, how many there are, or what they do. You can add new observers without touching the subject at all.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">5. Classes should be open for extension, but closed for modification.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> you should be able to add new behaviour by adding new code, not by editing existing, working, tested code.</p><p class="learn-p">Known as the <b>Open-Closed Principle</b>. The tell that you are violating it is a growing <code>if/else</code> or <code>switch</code> on a type tag &mdash; every new case means editing that function again. Replacing that switch with polymorphism is the single most common refactor in this interview round.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">6. Depend upon abstractions. Do not depend upon concrete classes.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> both high-level and low-level code should depend on interfaces in the middle.</p><p class="learn-p">The <b>Dependency Inversion Principle</b>. "Inversion" because naively your high-level <code>OrderService</code> would depend on the low-level <code>MySQLRepo</code>; instead you define a <code>Repository</code> interface owned by the high-level code, and the database class implements it. The arrow of dependency now points <i>up</i>, and you can swap the database without touching the business logic.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">7. Principle of Least Knowledge &mdash; talk only to your immediate friends.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> don\'t reach through one object to get at another.</p><div class="learn-code">// Violates it - now you depend on Order, Customer AND Address\norder.getCustomer().getAddress().getCity()\n\n// Better: ask the object you know, for what you need\norder.shippingCity()</div><p class="learn-p">Also called the <b>Law of Demeter</b>. The chain is fragile: any of those three classes changing breaks your code. Spotting a "train wreck" call chain in a design discussion is a nice catch.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">8. Don\'t call us, we\'ll call you (the Hollywood Principle).</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> the framework calls your code; your code doesn\'t call the framework.</p><p class="learn-p">This is what <b>Template Method</b> does: the base class owns the algorithm\'s skeleton and calls down into your overridden steps at the right moments. You never call the skeleton yourself.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">9. A class should have only one reason to change.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>In plain English:</b> each class does one job.</p><p class="learn-p">The <b>Single Responsibility Principle</b>. The practical test: describe the class in one sentence. If you need the word "and", it probably wants splitting. A class that formats a report <i>and</i> emails it has two reasons to change &mdash; a formatting change and a delivery change &mdash; and they will fight.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">How to actually use these in the room</div><p class="learn-p">Do not recite them. Use them as <b>justifications</b>. When you make a design decision, name the principle behind it in half a sentence:</p><p class="learn-p">"I\'ll put the discount rules behind an interface &mdash; that\'s the part most likely to change, and it keeps the order code closed for modification when marketing invents a new promotion."</p><p class="learn-p">You have just cited principles 1 and 5 without sounding like a textbook.</p></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'The patterns you will actually use',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">Seven patterns cover the overwhelming majority of what this round asks for. Each one below has: the problem it solves, the book\'s definition, working C++, and &mdash; just as important &mdash; when <b>not</b> to reach for it.</p><div class="learn-warn"><b>The warning that matters most.</b> The commonest way to fail a design round is to bolt on patterns to show you know them. A Singleton nobody needed, a Factory producing one product, an Observer with one observer. Interviewers read that as cargo-culting. <b>Name a pattern only when you can say what would go wrong without it.</b></div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Strategy</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The problem:</b> you have a family of interchangeable algorithms &mdash; several ways to calculate shipping, several ways to sort, several discount rules &mdash; and you keep editing a growing <code>if/else</code> every time a new one appears.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. Strategy lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it."</i></p><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nclass ShippingStrategy {\npublic:\n    virtual ~ShippingStrategy() = default;\n    virtual double cost(double weightKg, double distanceKm) const = 0;\n};\n\nclass StandardShipping : public ShippingStrategy {\npublic:\n    double cost(double w, double d) const override { return 3.0 + 0.5 * w + 0.01 * d; }\n};\n\nclass ExpressShipping : public ShippingStrategy {\npublic:\n    double cost(double w, double d) const override { return 9.0 + 1.2 * w + 0.03 * d; }\n};\n\nclass Order {\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;ShippingStrategy&gt; shipping_;\npublic:\n    explicit Order(std::unique_ptr&lt;ShippingStrategy&gt; s) : shipping_(std::move(s)) {}\n\n    // swappable at RUNTIME - the thing inheritance cannot do\n    void setShipping(std::unique_ptr&lt;ShippingStrategy&gt; s) { shipping_ = std::move(s); }\n\n    double shippingCost(double w, double d) const { return shipping_-&gt;cost(w, d); }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Where it shows up in this round:</b> pricing, discounts, ranking, routing, retry policies, export formats. Any time the interviewer says "and we might want to add other kinds of X later", they are asking for Strategy.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Don\'t use it when</b> there is exactly one algorithm and no realistic prospect of a second. And remember <b>Class design in C++</b>: if the strategy is one stateless function, <code>std::function</code> is the lighter C++ answer.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Observer</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The problem:</b> when something happens, several unrelated parts of the system need to react &mdash; and you do not want the thing that changed to know about all of them.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all of its dependents are notified and updated automatically."</i></p><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n\nclass OrderObserver {\npublic:\n    virtual ~OrderObserver() = default;\n    virtual void onOrderPlaced(const std::string&amp; orderId) = 0;\n};\n\nclass OrderService {\n    // weak_ptr: we watch observers, we do NOT own them, and we can tell\n    // when one has been destroyed. This is the C++-specific bit.\n    std::vector&lt;std::weak_ptr&lt;OrderObserver&gt;&gt; observers_;\npublic:\n    void subscribe(std::shared_ptr&lt;OrderObserver&gt; o) {\n        observers_.push_back(std::move(o));\n    }\n\n    void placeOrder(const std::string&amp; orderId) {\n        // ... persist the order ...\n        notify(orderId);\n    }\n\nprivate:\n    void notify(const std::string&amp; orderId) {\n        // prune dead observers while notifying the live ones\n        auto dead = std::remove_if(observers_.begin(), observers_.end(),\n            [&amp;](const std::weak_ptr&lt;OrderObserver&gt;&amp; w) {\n                if (auto o = w.lock()) { o-&gt;onOrderPlaced(orderId); return false; }\n                return true;                       // expired -> remove\n            });\n        observers_.erase(dead, observers_.end());\n    }\n};\n\nclass EmailNotifier : public OrderObserver {\npublic:\n    void onOrderPlaced(const std::string&amp; id) override { /* send email */ }\n};\nclass InventoryUpdater : public OrderObserver {\npublic:\n    void onOrderPlaced(const std::string&amp; id) override { /* decrement stock */ }\n};</div><div class="learn-tip"><b>Say the <code>weak_ptr</code> reasoning out loud.</b> "I hold observers by <code>weak_ptr</code> so the subject doesn\'t keep them alive &mdash; otherwise you get a leak, or a cycle if an observer also holds the subject. Locking the weak pointer also gives me a natural place to prune dead ones." In Java the collector handles this; in C++ it is a design decision, and noticing it is a strong signal.</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Where it shows up:</b> "when an order is placed we also need to email the customer, update inventory, and notify the warehouse." Three things reacting to one event is the tell.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Don\'t use it when</b> there is one listener and there always will be &mdash; a direct call is clearer. Also be honest about the downside: notification order is not guaranteed and debugging becomes harder, because control flow is no longer local.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Decorator</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The problem:</b> you need to add optional responsibilities in arbitrary combinations. With inheritance you would need a subclass per combination &mdash; the class explosion the book opens with.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"attaches additional responsibilities to an object dynamically. Decorators provide a flexible alternative to subclassing for extending functionality."</i></p><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nclass Beverage {\npublic:\n    virtual ~Beverage() = default;\n    virtual std::string description() const = 0;\n    virtual double cost() const = 0;\n};\n\nclass Espresso : public Beverage {\npublic:\n    std::string description() const override { return "Espresso"; }\n    double cost() const override { return 1.99; }\n};\n\n// The decorator IS-A Beverage and HAS-A Beverage. That dual nature is the whole trick:\n// being a Beverage lets it stand in anywhere; having one lets it wrap.\nclass CondimentDecorator : public Beverage {\nprotected:\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Beverage&gt; inner_;\npublic:\n    explicit CondimentDecorator(std::unique_ptr&lt;Beverage&gt; b) : inner_(std::move(b)) {}\n};\n\nclass Mocha : public CondimentDecorator {\npublic:\n    using CondimentDecorator::CondimentDecorator;\n    std::string description() const override { return inner_-&gt;description() + ", Mocha"; }\n    double cost() const override { return inner_-&gt;cost() + 0.20; }\n};\n\nclass Whip : public CondimentDecorator {\npublic:\n    using CondimentDecorator::CondimentDecorator;\n    std::string description() const override { return inner_-&gt;description() + ", Whip"; }\n    double cost() const override { return inner_-&gt;cost() + 0.10; }\n};\n\n// Espresso, Mocha, Mocha, Whip - stacked at runtime, no combinatorial subclasses\ninline std::unique_ptr&lt;Beverage&gt; makeDrink() {\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Beverage&gt; d = std::make_unique&lt;Espresso&gt;();\n    d = std::make_unique&lt;Mocha&gt;(std::move(d));\n    d = std::make_unique&lt;Mocha&gt;(std::move(d));\n    d = std::make_unique&lt;Whip&gt;(std::move(d));\n    return d;\n}</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Where it shows up:</b> pricing with add-ons, middleware chains (logging wrapping auth wrapping the handler), compression or encryption wrapping a stream, feature toppings.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Don\'t use it when</b> the combinations are few and fixed &mdash; three flags on a struct beat a wrapper tower. The honest downside: a stack of decorators is hard to debug and you lose the concrete type of what is inside.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Factory Method</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The problem:</b> your code needs to create objects, but which concrete class depends on context &mdash; and you do not want <code>new ConcreteThing</code> scattered everywhere, because that is depending on a concrete class (principle 6).</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate."</i></p><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nclass Pizza {\npublic:\n    virtual ~Pizza() = default;\n    virtual void prepare() = 0;\n};\nclass NYCheesePizza : public Pizza { public: void prepare() override {} };\nclass ChicagoCheesePizza : public Pizza { public: void prepare() override {} };\n\nclass PizzaStore {\npublic:\n    virtual ~PizzaStore() = default;\n\n    // the algorithm lives here, once\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Pizza&gt; orderPizza(const std::string&amp; type) {\n        auto pizza = createPizza(type);   // ...but the CREATION is deferred\n        pizza-&gt;prepare();\n        return pizza;\n    }\nprotected:\n    // the factory method: subclasses decide what gets built\n    virtual std::unique_ptr&lt;Pizza&gt; createPizza(const std::string&amp; type) = 0;\n};\n\nclass NYPizzaStore : public PizzaStore {\nprotected:\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Pizza&gt; createPizza(const std::string&amp;) override {\n        return std::make_unique&lt;NYCheesePizza&gt;();\n    }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Simpler cousin worth naming:</b> a <b>static factory function</b> &mdash; one function with a switch that returns the right <code>unique_ptr</code>. Not a GoF pattern, but often the right answer, and saying "a simple factory function is enough here" shows judgement rather than pattern-hunger.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Abstract Factory</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The problem:</b> you need to create <i>families</i> of related objects that must be used together, and mixing families would be a bug.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"provides an interface for creating families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes."</i></p><div class="learn-code">// the product interfaces, and one family\'s concrete versions\nclass Dough  { public: virtual ~Dough()  = default; };\nclass Sauce  { public: virtual ~Sauce()  = default; };\nclass Cheese { public: virtual ~Cheese() = default; };\nclass ThinCrust : public Dough  {};\nclass Marinara  : public Sauce  {};\nclass Reggiano  : public Cheese {};\n\nclass IngredientFactory {\npublic:\n    virtual ~IngredientFactory() = default;\n    virtual std::unique_ptr&lt;Dough&gt;  createDough()  = 0;\n    virtual std::unique_ptr&lt;Sauce&gt;  createSauce()  = 0;\n    virtual std::unique_ptr&lt;Cheese&gt; createCheese() = 0;\n};\n\nclass NYIngredientFactory : public IngredientFactory {\npublic:\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Dough&gt;  createDough()  override { return std::make_unique&lt;ThinCrust&gt;(); }\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Sauce&gt;  createSauce()  override { return std::make_unique&lt;Marinara&gt;(); }\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Cheese&gt; createCheese() override { return std::make_unique&lt;Reggiano&gt;(); }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Factory Method vs Abstract Factory</b> &mdash; they will ask. Factory Method creates <i>one</i> product and varies it through <b>inheritance</b> (a subclass overrides the method). Abstract Factory creates a <i>family</i> of products and varies it through <b>composition</b> (you hand the object a different factory). One product, one method; a family, an object.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Command</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The problem:</b> you want to treat "do this thing" as an object &mdash; so you can queue it, log it, schedule it, or undo it.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"encapsulates a request as an object, thereby letting you parameterize other objects with different requests, queue or log requests, and support undoable operations."</i></p><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nclass Command {\npublic:\n    virtual ~Command() = default;\n    virtual void execute() = 0;\n    virtual void undo()    = 0;      // undo is what makes Command earn its keep\n};\n\nclass Light {\npublic:\n    void on()  {}\n    void off() {}\n};\n\nclass LightOnCommand : public Command {\n    Light&amp; light_;                    // non-owning: the receiver outlives the command\npublic:\n    explicit LightOnCommand(Light&amp; l) : light_(l) {}\n    void execute() override { light_.on();  }\n    void undo()    override { light_.off(); }\n};\n\nclass RemoteControl {\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Command&gt;&gt; history_;\npublic:\n    void submit(std::unique_ptr&lt;Command&gt; c) {\n        c-&gt;execute();\n        history_.push_back(std::move(c));\n    }\n    void undoLast() {\n        if (history_.empty()) return;\n        history_.back()-&gt;undo();\n        history_.pop_back();\n    }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Where it shows up:</b> undo/redo, job queues, transactional operations, request logging for replay after a crash, macro recording. If the interviewer mentions <b>undo</b> or <b>a queue of work</b>, this is the pattern.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Singleton</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The problem:</b> exactly one instance must exist &mdash; one configuration object, one connection pool.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"ensures a class has only one instance, and provides a global point of access to it."</i></p><div class="learn-code">class Config {\npublic:\n    // "Meyers singleton": thread-safe initialisation is guaranteed by the\n    // standard since C++11, so no locking is needed. Prefer this always.\n    static Config&amp; instance() {\n        static Config c;      // constructed once, on first use\n        return c;\n    }\n    Config(const Config&amp;)            = delete;   // no copying\n    Config&amp; operator=(const Config&amp;) = delete;\nprivate:\n    Config() = default;\n};</div><div class="learn-tip"><b>Say the criticism before they do.</b> "It\'s a Meyers singleton, which is thread-safe since C++11. That said, singletons are effectively global state &mdash; they hide dependencies and make testing hard, because you can\'t substitute a fake. In a real design I\'d usually prefer to create one instance at startup and inject it as a dependency."<br><br>Volunteering the downside of the pattern you just used is one of the strongest moves available in a design interview.</div></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'The rest of the pattern catalogue',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">Less frequent in this round, but each has a scenario where it is clearly the right answer. Know the one-line definition and the trigger for each.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Adapter</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"converts the interface of a class into another interface the clients expect. Adapter lets classes work together that couldn\'t otherwise because of incompatible interfaces."</i></p><p class="learn-p"><b>Trigger:</b> a third-party library or legacy class whose interface does not match yours, and you cannot change it.</p><div class="learn-code">// Their interface (can\'t change it)\nclass LegacyPaymentGateway {\npublic:\n    bool doPayment(const char* cardNo, int cents) { return true; }\n};\n\n// Our interface\nclass PaymentProcessor {\npublic:\n    virtual ~PaymentProcessor() = default;\n    virtual bool charge(const std::string&amp; card, double amount) = 0;\n};\n\nclass LegacyGatewayAdapter : public PaymentProcessor {\n    LegacyPaymentGateway&amp; gateway_;\npublic:\n    explicit LegacyGatewayAdapter(LegacyPaymentGateway&amp; g) : gateway_(g) {}\n    bool charge(const std::string&amp; card, double amount) override {\n        return gateway_.doPayment(card.c_str(), static_cast&lt;int&gt;(amount * 100));\n    }\n};</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Facade</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"provides a unified interface to a set of interfaces in a subsystem. Facade defines a higher-level interface that makes the subsystem easier to use."</i></p><p class="learn-p"><b>Trigger:</b> a client has to orchestrate six objects in the right order to do one common task. <b>Adapter vs Facade:</b> Adapter changes an interface to match what a client expects; Facade simplifies a complicated one. Different intent, similar mechanics.</p><div class="learn-code">// collaborators, elided. PaymentProcessor is the interface from the Adapter example\n// above - so the adapted legacy gateway drops straight in here.\nclass Cart { public: double total() const { return 0.0; } };\nclass InventoryService { public: bool reserve(const Cart&amp;){ return true; } void release(const Cart&amp;){} };\nclass ShippingService  { public: void schedule(const Cart&amp;){} };\n\nclass OrderFacade {\n    InventoryService&amp; inventory_;\n    PaymentProcessor&amp; payment_;      // an interface, so any implementation works\n    ShippingService&amp;  shipping_;\npublic:\n    OrderFacade(InventoryService&amp; i, PaymentProcessor&amp; p, ShippingService&amp; s)\n        : inventory_(i), payment_(p), shipping_(s) {}\n\n    // one call instead of the client knowing the whole dance\n    bool placeOrder(const Cart&amp; cart, const std::string&amp; card) {\n        if (!inventory_.reserve(cart)) return false;\n        if (!payment_.charge(card, cart.total())) {\n            inventory_.release(cart);            // undo the reservation on failure\n            return false;\n        }\n        shipping_.schedule(cart);\n        return true;\n    }\n};</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Template Method</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a method, deferring some steps to subclasses. Template Method lets subclasses redefine certain steps of an algorithm without changing the algorithm\'s structure."</i></p><p class="learn-p"><b>Trigger:</b> several processes that follow the same sequence but differ in a couple of steps &mdash; importing CSV vs XML, or any "fetch, validate, transform, save" pipeline.</p><div class="learn-code">struct Row {};                    // a parsed record\n\nclass DataImporter {\npublic:\n    virtual ~DataImporter() = default;\n\n    // non-virtual: the skeleton is fixed. Subclasses cannot reorder it.\n    void import(const std::string&amp; path) {\n        auto raw  = read(path);        // varies\n        auto rows = parse(raw);        // varies\n        validate(rows);                // shared\n        save(rows);                    // shared\n        if (shouldArchive()) archive(path);   // a "hook" with a default\n    }\nprotected:\n    virtual std::string read(const std::string&amp; path) = 0;\n    virtual std::vector&lt;Row&gt; parse(const std::string&amp; raw) = 0;\n    virtual bool shouldArchive() const { return false; }   // hook: optional override\nprivate:\n    void validate(const std::vector&lt;Row&gt;&amp;) {}\n    void save(const std::vector&lt;Row&gt;&amp;) {}\n    void archive(const std::string&amp;) {}\n};</div><p class="learn-p">Note the <b>hook</b> &mdash; a virtual with a default implementation that subclasses may override but need not. And note this is the Hollywood Principle in action: the base class calls down into you.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Template Method vs Strategy:</b> Template Method varies <i>steps within</i> a fixed algorithm using inheritance. Strategy swaps the <i>whole</i> algorithm using composition.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Iterator</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"provides a way to access the elements of an aggregate object sequentially without exposing its underlying representation."</i></p><p class="learn-p">In C++ this is mostly already solved: the STL is built on iterators, so the idiomatic answer is to expose <code>begin()</code> and <code>end()</code> and let range-for work.</p><div class="learn-code">struct Song {};\n\nclass Playlist {\n    std::vector&lt;Song&gt; songs_;\npublic:\n    // Now: for (const auto&amp; s : playlist) { ... }  - and every STL algorithm works.\n    auto begin()       { return songs_.begin(); }\n    auto end()         { return songs_.end();   }\n    auto begin() const { return songs_.begin(); }\n    auto end()   const { return songs_.end();   }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The point to make:</b> the caller iterates without knowing whether you store a <code>vector</code>, a <code>list</code> or a database cursor. You can change the container without breaking anyone.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Composite</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"allows you to compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. Composite lets clients treat individual objects and compositions of objects uniformly."</i></p><p class="learn-p"><b>Trigger:</b> anything tree-shaped &mdash; a filesystem, a menu with submenus, an org chart, nested UI components, a bill of materials.</p><div class="learn-code">class FileNode {\npublic:\n    virtual ~FileNode() = default;\n    virtual std::string name() const = 0;\n    virtual long        size() const = 0;     // leaf and composite answer identically\n};\n\nclass File : public FileNode {\n    std::string name_; long size_;\npublic:\n    File(std::string n, long s) : name_(std::move(n)), size_(s) {}\n    std::string name() const override { return name_; }\n    long        size() const override { return size_; }\n};\n\nclass Directory : public FileNode {\n    std::string name_;\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;FileNode&gt;&gt; children_;\npublic:\n    explicit Directory(std::string n) : name_(std::move(n)) {}\n    void add(std::unique_ptr&lt;FileNode&gt; c) { children_.push_back(std::move(c)); }\n    std::string name() const override { return name_; }\n    long size() const override {                 // recursion, transparent to the caller\n        long total = 0;\n        for (const auto&amp; c : children_) total += c-&gt;size();\n        return total;\n    }\n};</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">State</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes. The object will appear to change its class."</i></p><p class="learn-p"><b>Trigger:</b> a big <code>switch (status)</code> repeated in several methods. An order that is Pending, Paid, Shipped, Delivered, Cancelled, where what each operation does depends on which state it is in.</p><div class="learn-code">class OrderContext;               // forward declaration is enough for a reference\n\nclass OrderState {\npublic:\n    virtual ~OrderState() = default;\n    virtual void pay(OrderContext&amp;)    {}   // by default, invalid transitions do nothing\n    virtual void cancel(OrderContext&amp;) {}\n    virtual const char* name() const = 0;\n};\n\nclass PendingState : public OrderState {\npublic:\n    void pay(OrderContext&amp; ctx) override;      // -> PaidState\n    void cancel(OrderContext&amp; ctx) override;   // -> CancelledState\n    const char* name() const override { return "PENDING"; }\n};\n\nclass ShippedState : public OrderState {\npublic:\n    // pay() and cancel() intentionally not overridden: you cannot cancel a shipped order.\n    const char* name() const override { return "SHIPPED"; }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>State vs Strategy</b> &mdash; the book calls them "twins separated at birth", and it is a favourite interview question. Structurally identical; the difference is intent and who decides. With <b>Strategy</b>, the client chooses the algorithm and it normally stays put. With <b>State</b>, the objects themselves drive the transitions, so the behaviour changes over the object\'s lifetime without the client doing anything.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Proxy</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The book:</b> <i>"provides a surrogate or placeholder for another object to control access to it."</i></p><p class="learn-p"><b>Trigger:</b> you want to add access control, caching, lazy loading or remote calls without changing the real object or its callers. Flavours: <b>virtual proxy</b> (create the expensive thing on first use), <b>protection proxy</b> (check permissions), <b>remote proxy</b> (a local stand-in for something on another machine), <b>caching proxy</b>.</p><div class="learn-code">class Image {\npublic:\n    virtual ~Image() = default;\n    virtual void display() const = 0;\n};\n\nclass RealImage : public Image {          // expensive: loads from disk on construction\npublic:\n    explicit RealImage(std::string path) {}\n    void display() const override {}\n};\n\nclass ImageProxy : public Image {\n    std::string path_;\n    mutable std::unique_ptr&lt;RealImage&gt; real_;   // mutable: created inside a const method\npublic:\n    explicit ImageProxy(std::string p) : path_(std::move(p)) {}\n    void display() const override {\n        if (!real_) real_ = std::make_unique&lt;RealImage&gt;(path_);  // load on first use only\n        real_-&gt;display();\n    }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Proxy vs Decorator</b> &mdash; also asked. Both wrap an object with the same interface. Decorator <i>adds behaviour</i>; Proxy <i>controls access</i> and normally does not change what the operation means.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Compound: MVC</div><p class="learn-p">The book\'s finale: Model-View-Controller is not one pattern but several working together.</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Model</b> notifies views of changes &mdash; that is <b>Observer</b>.</li><li><b>Controller</b> is a <b>Strategy</b> for the view: swap the controller and the same view behaves differently.</li><li>Nested view components form a <b>Composite</b>.</li></ul><p class="learn-p">Worth a sentence if you design anything with a UI, because it demonstrates that patterns combine &mdash; which is the book\'s real thesis.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The comparison table they will probe</div><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Confusable pair</th><th>The distinction in one line</th></tr><tr><td>Strategy vs State</td><td>Client picks the algorithm vs the objects transition themselves</td></tr><tr><td>Strategy vs Template Method</td><td>Swap the whole algorithm (composition) vs vary steps inside it (inheritance)</td></tr><tr><td>Decorator vs Proxy</td><td>Adds behaviour vs controls access</td></tr><tr><td>Decorator vs Inheritance</td><td>Runtime and stackable vs compile-time and fixed</td></tr><tr><td>Adapter vs Facade</td><td>Convert an interface vs simplify a subsystem</td></tr><tr><td>Factory Method vs Abstract Factory</td><td>One product via inheritance vs a family via composition</td></tr><tr><td>Observer vs Mediator</td><td>Broadcast to many vs centralise many-to-many coordination</td></tr></table></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Data modeling and schema design',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">The brief for this area: "tables, columns, sensible relationships between tables, and primary and foreign keys." That is a precise list, so hit every item explicitly &mdash; name your primary keys, name your foreign keys, and say what each relationship is.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Step 1: entities and relationships</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Cardinality</b> &mdash; how many of one thing relate to how many of another. The three cases are one-to-one, one-to-many, and many-to-many.</p><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>How to implement it</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td><b>One-to-many</b> (1:N)</td><td>Foreign key on the <b>many</b> side, pointing at the one</td><td>A branch has many copies &rarr; <code>copy.branch_id</code></td></tr><tr><td><b>Many-to-many</b> (M:N)</td><td>A separate <b>join table</b> holding both foreign keys</td><td>Books and authors &rarr; <code>book_author(book_id, author_id)</code></td></tr><tr><td><b>One-to-one</b> (1:1)</td><td>Usually the same table. Split only to isolate rarely-used or sensitive columns</td><td>User and user_credentials</td></tr></table><div class="learn-tip"><b>The move that earns marks:</b> when you spot a many-to-many, say <i>"that\'s many-to-many, so it needs a join table &mdash; and the join table is usually a real entity with its own data."</i><br><br>A student enrolling on a course is not just <code>(student_id, course_id)</code>; the enrolment has a date, a grade, a status. Recognising that the join table wants columns of its own is the difference between a schema that works and one that has to be rebuilt.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Step 2: keys</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Primary key</b> &mdash; the column (or columns) that uniquely identify a row.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Surrogate</b> keys are meaningless ids you generate (an auto-increment integer or a UUID). <b>Natural</b> keys are real-world values (an ISBN, an email). Prefer surrogate: natural keys have an unfortunate habit of changing, and then every foreign key referencing them has to change too. Keep the natural key as a <code>UNIQUE</code> column instead.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Foreign key</b> &mdash; a column holding another table\'s primary key, with the database enforcing that the referenced row actually exists.</p><p class="learn-p">Also decide what happens on delete: <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> (delete the children too), <code>RESTRICT</code> (refuse while children exist), or <code>SET NULL</code>. Saying which one you want, and why, is a detail most candidates skip.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Auto-increment integer vs UUID:</b> integers are compact and index well but leak volume information and are awkward across distributed systems; UUIDs are globally unique and can be generated client-side (which pairs nicely with idempotency keys) but are larger and randomly ordered, which hurts index locality. Have the trade-off ready.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Step 3: normalisation</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Normalisation</b> &mdash; organising columns so each fact is stored in exactly one place, so it cannot disagree with itself.</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>1NF</b> &mdash; no repeating groups; each cell holds a single value. No <code>phone1, phone2, phone3</code> columns and no comma-separated lists.</li><li><b>2NF</b> &mdash; 1NF, and every non-key column depends on the <i>whole</i> primary key. Only bites when the key is composite.</li><li><b>3NF</b> &mdash; 2NF, and no non-key column depends on another non-key column. If you store <code>city</code> and <code>postcode</code> and the postcode determines the city, that is a violation.</li></ul><p class="learn-p"><b>The one-liner to remember:</b> <i>"every non-key column depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key."</i> 3NF is the target for a normal transactional schema.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Then say when you would break it</b></p><p class="learn-p">Normalisation prevents update anomalies but costs joins. Denormalise deliberately when reads dominate and the data is stable:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Counters</b> &mdash; storing <code>comment_count</code> on a post beats counting a million rows, at the cost of keeping it in step.</li><li><b>Point-in-time snapshots</b> &mdash; and this one is not really denormalisation at all. An order line must store <b>the price at the time of purchase</b>, not join to the product\'s current price. If the product\'s price changes next week, every historical invoice must not change with it. <b>This is the single most commonly missed modelling point in e-commerce questions</b>, and raising it unprompted is a strong signal.</li></ul></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Step 4: column types and the details that catch people</div><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Data</th><th>Use</th><th>Not</th></tr><tr><td>Money</td><td><code>DECIMAL(12,2)</code>, or an integer count of minor units</td><td><code>FLOAT</code> &mdash; binary floating point cannot represent 0.10 exactly, so sums drift</td></tr><tr><td>Timestamps</td><td><code>TIMESTAMPTZ</code>, always stored UTC</td><td>Local time with no zone &mdash; unrecoverable ambiguity</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed sets</td><td>An <code>ENUM</code>, or a lookup table with a foreign key</td><td>Free-text status strings</td></tr><tr><td>Identifiers</td><td><code>BIGINT</code> or <code>UUID</code></td><td><code>INT</code>, if there is any chance of exceeding 2.1 billion</td></tr></table><p class="learn-p">The <b>money as float</b> point is worth stating out loud in any commerce question. It is a real bug, it is common, and knowing it reads as production experience.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Step 5: indexes</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Index</b> &mdash; a sorted side-structure that lets the database find matching rows without scanning the whole table &mdash; like a book\'s index.</p><p class="learn-p">The trade: reads get faster, writes get slower (every insert must update every index), and disk usage grows. So index deliberately, not everywhere.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>What to index:</b> every foreign key (you will join on it); columns in <code>WHERE</code> clauses you run often; columns you sort by.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Composite index and column order</b> &mdash; an index on several columns at once. The order matters enormously.</p><p class="learn-p">An index on <code>(member_id, status)</code> helps a query filtering on <code>member_id</code> alone, and one filtering on both &mdash; but <b>not</b> one filtering on <code>status</code> alone. It is like a phone book sorted by surname then first name: useless for finding everyone called "James" if you do not know the surname. Say "leftmost prefix rule" and you will have said something most candidates cannot.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Step 6: constraints and concurrency</div><p class="learn-p">Push correctness into the database where you can, because application code has bugs and races:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><code>NOT NULL</code> on everything that genuinely must be present.</li><li><code>UNIQUE</code> on natural keys &mdash; and this is how you prevent double-booking. A unique constraint on <code>(copy_id) WHERE status = \'ACTIVE\'</code> (a partial unique index) makes it <i>physically impossible</i> for one copy to have two active loans, no matter what the application does.</li><li><code>CHECK</code> for invariants, e.g. <code>CHECK (due_at &gt; borrowed_at)</code>.</li></ul><div class="learn-tip"><b>The double-booking answer</b><br>Almost every problem in this round has a "two people grab the last one" moment. Have this ready:<br><br>"The read-then-write is a race: two requests both see the copy as available and both insert a loan. Three defences, and I\'d use more than one. First, a <b>transaction</b> with the check and the insert inside it. Second, either <b>pessimistic</b> locking (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> on the copy row, blocking the second request) or <b>optimistic</b> locking (a version column; the second write fails and retries). Third, and most robust, a <b>unique constraint</b> so that even if the logic is wrong the database refuses the second row &mdash; and I\'d return 409 Conflict when it does."<br><br>Pessimistic suits high contention and short transactions; optimistic suits low contention. Naming both and choosing is what they want.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Step 7: the questions that show seniority</div><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Soft deletes</b> &mdash; a <code>deleted_at</code> column instead of really deleting, so you keep history and can undo. Cost: every query must remember to filter it.</li><li><b>Audit trail</b> &mdash; <code>created_at</code>, <code>updated_at</code>, <code>created_by</code> on anything a human edits.</li><li><b>History vs current state</b> &mdash; does the business need to know what a row used to say? If so, an events or history table, not just an updated row.</li><li><b>Multi-tenancy</b> &mdash; if several customers share the database, a <code>tenant_id</code> on every table and in every index.</li></ul></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Problem 1: Library system',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">The full 45 minutes, written out. This continues the scoping from <b>Turning a vague prompt into a design</b>.</p><div class="learn-tip"><b>Prompt:</b> "Design a library system." Scope agreed earlier: physical books, multiple branches, borrow / return / reserve, fines on late return, ~50k members, read-heavy, and a copy must never be lent twice.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Deep dive 1: the schema</div><p class="learn-p">Recall the key insight: <b>a title is not a copy</b>.</p><div class="learn-code">member\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  email          TEXT         UNIQUE NOT NULL\n  name           TEXT         NOT NULL\n  joined_at      TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL\n  status         ENUM(\'ACTIVE\',\'SUSPENDED\') NOT NULL\n\nbranch\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  name           TEXT         NOT NULL\n  address        TEXT\n\ntitle                             -- the work: "The Hobbit"\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  isbn           TEXT         UNIQUE          -- natural key kept as UNIQUE, not as PK\n  name           TEXT         NOT NULL\n  published_year SMALLINT\n\nauthor\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  name           TEXT         NOT NULL\n\ntitle_author                      -- M:N join. A book has many authors; an author many books.\n  title_id       BIGINT       FK -> title(id)   ON DELETE CASCADE\n  author_id      BIGINT       FK -> author(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE\n  PRIMARY KEY (title_id, author_id)\n\ncopy                              -- a physical object on a shelf\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  title_id       BIGINT       FK -> title(id)   NOT NULL\n  branch_id      BIGINT       FK -> branch(id)  NOT NULL\n  barcode        TEXT         UNIQUE NOT NULL\n  condition      ENUM(\'GOOD\',\'WORN\',\'DAMAGED\') NOT NULL DEFAULT \'GOOD\'\n\nloan                              -- the join between member and copy, with its own data\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  copy_id        BIGINT       FK -> copy(id)    NOT NULL\n  member_id      BIGINT       FK -> member(id)  NOT NULL\n  borrowed_at    TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL\n  due_at         TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL\n  returned_at    TIMESTAMPTZ  NULL              -- NULL means still out\n  CHECK (due_at &gt; borrowed_at)\n\nreservation                       -- a hold on a TITLE, not a copy: any copy will do\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  title_id       BIGINT       FK -> title(id)   NOT NULL\n  member_id      BIGINT       FK -> member(id)  NOT NULL\n  created_at     TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL\n  status         ENUM(\'WAITING\',\'READY\',\'FULFILLED\',\'CANCELLED\') NOT NULL\n\nfine\n  id             BIGINT       PK\n  loan_id        BIGINT       FK -> loan(id)    NOT NULL\n  amount         DECIMAL(8,2) NOT NULL          -- DECIMAL, never FLOAT\n  assessed_at    TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL\n  paid_at        TIMESTAMPTZ  NULL</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Indexes, and why each one exists</b></p><div class="learn-code">CREATE INDEX idx_copy_title_branch ON copy(title_id, branch_id);\n    -- "which copies of this title are at this branch?" - the main catalogue query\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_loan_member_active ON loan(member_id) WHERE returned_at IS NULL;\n    -- "what does this member currently have out?" - a partial index, so it stays small\n\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX uniq_active_loan_per_copy ON loan(copy_id) WHERE returned_at IS NULL;\n    -- THE IMPORTANT ONE. A copy can have at most one open loan. The database now makes\n    -- double-lending physically impossible, whatever the application code does.</div><div class="learn-tip">Point at that last index and say: "This is how I\'d stop double-lending. Two concurrent borrow requests can both pass an availability check, but only one can insert the row &mdash; the second violates the unique index, and I turn that into a <b>409 Conflict</b>."</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Deep dive 2: the API</div><div class="learn-code">GET  /titles?q=hobbit&amp;branchId=3&amp;limit=20&amp;cursor=...\n     200 { "items":[ { "id":"tl_1","name":"The Hobbit","authors":["Tolkien"],\n                       "availableCopies": 2 } ], "nextCursor":"..." }\n\nGET  /titles/{titleId}/copies?branchId=3\n     200 { "items":[ { "id":"cp_88","barcode":"X1","status":"AVAILABLE" } ] }\n\nPOST /loans                    Idempotency-Key: &lt;uuid&gt;\n     { "copyId":"cp_88", "memberId":"mb_42" }\n     201 { "id":"ln_5", "dueAt":"2026-08-29T23:59:59Z", "status":"ACTIVE" }\n     409 { "error":{ "code":"COPY_ALREADY_ON_LOAN", ... } }\n     422 { "error":{ "code":"MEMBER_LOAN_LIMIT_REACHED", ... } }\n\nPOST /loans/{loanId}/return    -- an action, not a CRUD update\n     200 { "id":"ln_5", "returnedAt":"...", "fine": { "amount":"2.50" } }\n\nPOST /reservations             { "titleId":"tl_1", "memberId":"mb_42" }\n     201 { "id":"rs_9", "position": 3 }\n\nGET  /members/{id}/loans?status=active</div><p class="learn-p">Points to make aloud: <code>memberId</code> in the body is a simplification &mdash; in reality it comes from the auth token, and you never let a client claim to be someone else. Reservation is on the <i>title</i> because the member does not care which copy. And returning the fine in the return response saves the client a second call.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Third area, lighter: the classes</div><p class="learn-p">Where the varying behaviour lives: <b>fine calculation</b> (students, seniors and staff get different rules) and <b>loan policy</b> (how many books, how long). That is Strategy, twice.</p><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;chrono&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nusing Clock     = std::chrono::system_clock;\nusing TimePoint = Clock::time_point;\n\n// ---- Strategy 1: what a late return costs -------------------------------\nclass FinePolicy {\npublic:\n    virtual ~FinePolicy() = default;\n    virtual double fineFor(int daysLate) const = 0;\n};\n\nclass StandardFinePolicy : public FinePolicy {\npublic:\n    double fineFor(int daysLate) const override {\n        return daysLate &lt;= 0 ? 0.0 : 0.50 * daysLate;\n    }\n};\n\nclass CappedFinePolicy : public FinePolicy {\n    double perDay_, cap_;\npublic:\n    CappedFinePolicy(double perDay, double cap) : perDay_(perDay), cap_(cap) {}\n    double fineFor(int daysLate) const override {\n        if (daysLate &lt;= 0) return 0.0;\n        double f = perDay_ * daysLate;\n        return f &gt; cap_ ? cap_ : f;\n    }\n};\n\n// ---- Strategy 2: how long and how many ----------------------------------\nclass LoanPolicy {\npublic:\n    virtual ~LoanPolicy() = default;\n    virtual int loanDays()    const = 0;\n    virtual int maxConcurrent() const = 0;\n};\n\nclass AdultLoanPolicy : public LoanPolicy {\npublic:\n    int loanDays()      const override { return 14; }\n    int maxConcurrent() const override { return 10; }\n};\n\n// ---- The service ---------------------------------------------------------\nclass LoanRepository;      // interface, defined elsewhere - program to an interface\n\nclass LoanService {\n    LoanRepository&amp;             repo_;      // non-owning: injected, outlives us\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;FinePolicy&gt; fines_;\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;LoanPolicy&gt; policy_;\npublic:\n    LoanService(LoanRepository&amp; repo,\n                std::unique_ptr&lt;FinePolicy&gt; fines,\n                std::unique_ptr&lt;LoanPolicy&gt; policy)\n        : repo_(repo), fines_(std::move(fines)), policy_(std::move(policy)) {}\n\n    // Returns the new loan id. Throws ConflictError if the copy is already out.\n    long borrow(long copyId, long memberId);\n\n    double returnCopy(long loanId, TimePoint when);\n};</div><p class="learn-p">Note what is <i>not</i> here: no Singleton, no Factory, no Observer. Three would be pattern-stuffing. But mention the extension: "if we later need to email members when a reservation becomes ready, that\'s an Observer on the return event &mdash; email, SMS and push all subscribe without the loan code changing."</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Close with trade-offs</div><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>At 100&times; scale:</b> the catalogue search moves out of the relational database into a search index; the availability count becomes a maintained counter rather than a live <code>COUNT</code>.</li><li><b>Deliberately left out:</b> payments for fines, membership sign-up, staff tooling.</li><li><b>Would revisit:</b> whether <code>reservation</code> should hold a specific copy once one becomes free &mdash; that turns into a small queue problem.</li></ul></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Problem 2: Parking lot',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">The most-asked OO design question anywhere. It looks simple and it is really testing whether you can model variation cleanly.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Requirements (after asking)</div><div class="learn-code">IN SCOPE                                OUT OF SCOPE\n- multiple levels, many spots           - payments processing (assume a gateway)\n- spot types: motorcycle, compact,      - number-plate recognition\n  large, electric (with charger)        - reservations\n- vehicle takes a suitable spot\n- ticket on entry, fee on exit\n- different pricing rules\n\nSCALE: one site, ~2000 spots, low write rate.\nCONSISTENCY: a spot must never be double-assigned.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Class design (the deep dive here &mdash; this problem is behaviour-heavy)</div><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;optional&gt;\n#include &lt;chrono&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nenum class VehicleType { Motorcycle, Car, Bus, Electric };\nenum class SpotType    { Motorcycle, Compact, Large, Electric };\n\nclass Vehicle {\n    std::string plate_;\n    VehicleType type_;\npublic:\n    Vehicle(std::string plate, VehicleType t) : plate_(std::move(plate)), type_(t) {}\n    const std::string&amp; plate() const { return plate_; }\n    VehicleType type()         const { return type_; }\n};\n\nclass ParkingSpot {\n    long     id_;\n    SpotType type_;\n    bool     occupied_ = false;\npublic:\n    ParkingSpot(long id, SpotType t) : id_(id), type_(t) {}\n    long     id()   const { return id_; }\n    SpotType type() const { return type_; }\n    bool     free() const { return !occupied_; }\n    void occupy()  { occupied_ = true;  }\n    void release() { occupied_ = false; }\n\n    // Which vehicles fit here. Kept as data, not as an if-chain scattered around.\n    bool fits(VehicleType v) const {\n        switch (type_) {\n            case SpotType::Motorcycle: return v == VehicleType::Motorcycle;\n            case SpotType::Compact:    return v == VehicleType::Motorcycle || v == VehicleType::Car;\n            case SpotType::Large:      return true;\n            case SpotType::Electric:   return v == VehicleType::Electric;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Strategy #1: how we choose a spot</b></p><div class="learn-code">class SpotAllocationStrategy {\npublic:\n    virtual ~SpotAllocationStrategy() = default;\n    virtual std::optional&lt;long&gt; choose(const std::vector&lt;ParkingSpot&gt;&amp; spots,\n                                       VehicleType v) const = 0;\n};\n\nclass FirstFitStrategy : public SpotAllocationStrategy {\npublic:\n    std::optional&lt;long&gt; choose(const std::vector&lt;ParkingSpot&gt;&amp; spots,\n                               VehicleType v) const override {\n        for (const auto&amp; s : spots)\n            if (s.free() &amp;&amp; s.fits(v)) return s.id();\n        return std::nullopt;\n    }\n};\n\n// Prefer the tightest fit so big spots stay free for big vehicles.\nclass BestFitStrategy : public SpotAllocationStrategy {\npublic:\n    std::optional&lt;long&gt; choose(const std::vector&lt;ParkingSpot&gt;&amp; spots,\n                               VehicleType v) const override {\n        const ParkingSpot* best = nullptr;\n        for (const auto&amp; s : spots) {\n            if (!s.free() || !s.fits(v)) continue;\n            if (!best || static_cast&lt;int&gt;(s.type()) &lt; static_cast&lt;int&gt;(best-&gt;type()))\n                best = &amp;s;\n        }\n        return best ? std::optional&lt;long&gt;(best-&gt;id()) : std::nullopt;\n    }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Strategy #2: how we charge &mdash; and Decorator for the extras</b></p><div class="learn-code">class PricingStrategy {\npublic:\n    virtual ~PricingStrategy() = default;\n    virtual double price(std::chrono::minutes stay, VehicleType v) const = 0;\n};\n\nclass HourlyPricing : public PricingStrategy {\n    double perHour_;\npublic:\n    explicit HourlyPricing(double r) : perHour_(r) {}\n    double price(std::chrono::minutes stay, VehicleType) const override {\n        long hours = (stay.count() + 59) / 60;      // round up to the next hour\n        return perHour_ * static_cast&lt;double&gt;(hours);\n    }\n};\n\n// Decorator: surcharges and discounts stack in any combination,\n// without a subclass per combination.\nclass PricingDecorator : public PricingStrategy {\nprotected:\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;PricingStrategy&gt; inner_;\npublic:\n    explicit PricingDecorator(std::unique_ptr&lt;PricingStrategy&gt; i) : inner_(std::move(i)) {}\n};\n\nclass WeekendSurcharge : public PricingDecorator {\npublic:\n    using PricingDecorator::PricingDecorator;\n    double price(std::chrono::minutes s, VehicleType v) const override {\n        return inner_-&gt;price(s, v) * 1.25;\n    }\n};\n\nclass ElectricDiscount : public PricingDecorator {\npublic:\n    using PricingDecorator::PricingDecorator;\n    double price(std::chrono::minutes s, VehicleType v) const override {\n        double base = inner_-&gt;price(s, v);\n        return v == VehicleType::Electric ? base * 0.90 : base;\n    }\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The lot itself</b></p><div class="learn-code">class ParkingLot {\n    std::vector&lt;ParkingSpot&gt;                 spots_;\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;SpotAllocationStrategy&gt;  allocator_;\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;PricingStrategy&gt;         pricing_;\npublic:\n    ParkingLot(std::vector&lt;ParkingSpot&gt; spots,\n               std::unique_ptr&lt;SpotAllocationStrategy&gt; a,\n               std::unique_ptr&lt;PricingStrategy&gt; p)\n        : spots_(std::move(spots)), allocator_(std::move(a)), pricing_(std::move(p)) {}\n\n    std::optional&lt;long&gt; park(const Vehicle&amp; v);            // returns ticket id\n    double             unpark(long ticketId);              // returns fee\n};</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Schema (lighter, but do not skip it)</div><div class="learn-code">level    (id PK, lot_id FK, floor_number)\nspot     (id PK, level_id FK -> level(id), type ENUM, spot_number,\n          UNIQUE (level_id, spot_number))\nticket   (id PK, spot_id FK -> spot(id), plate TEXT, vehicle_type ENUM,\n          entered_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL, exited_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL,\n          amount DECIMAL(8,2) NULL)\n\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX uniq_open_ticket_per_spot ON ticket(spot_id) WHERE exited_at IS NULL;\n    -- same trick as the library: one open ticket per spot, enforced by the database</div><p class="learn-p">Note <b>spot occupancy is derived</b>, not stored twice: a spot is occupied exactly when it has an open ticket. Storing a separate <code>occupied</code> flag in the database as well means two sources of truth that can disagree. (The in-memory <code>ParkingSpot</code> caches it for speed; the database is authoritative.) Spotting that is a genuinely good modelling observation.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">API</div><div class="learn-code">POST /tickets           { "plate":"KA01AB1234", "vehicleType":"CAR" }\n     201 { "ticketId":"tk_9", "spotId":"sp_144", "enteredAt":"..." }\n     409 { "error":{ "code":"LOT_FULL" } }\nPOST /tickets/{id}/exit\n     200 { "amount":"7.50", "durationMinutes":95 }\nGET  /availability      200 { "MOTORCYCLE":12, "COMPACT":40, "LARGE":3, "ELECTRIC":0 }</div></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Problem 3: E-commerce cart and checkout',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">The one most likely to be asked at a retail company, and the one with the most classic traps.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Requirements</div><div class="learn-code">IN SCOPE                                OUT OF SCOPE\n- browse products, add to cart          - the payment gateway itself\n- promotions: % off, buy-2-get-1,       - shipping carrier integration\n  free shipping over a threshold        - returns\n- checkout creates an order\n- stock must not oversell\n\nSCALE: 1M products, read-heavy catalogue, spiky checkout traffic.\nCONSISTENCY: stock and order creation must be exact; catalogue can be slightly stale.</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The three traps, and how to walk into them deliberately</div><ol class="learn-list"><li><b>Price must be snapshotted.</b> <code>order_line</code> stores the price paid, not a join to the product\'s current price. Otherwise last year\'s invoices silently change when marketing runs a sale. State this as you write the table &mdash; it is the highest-value sentence in the problem.</li><li><b>Product is not Inventory.</b> Same abstract-vs-physical split as title/copy. A product is a catalogue entry; stock is per warehouse. And a <b>variant</b> (size, colour) is a third level that most candidates miss: you do not stock "T-shirt", you stock "T-shirt / Medium / Blue".</li><li><b>Overselling is a race.</b> Same defence as before: transaction, plus either a conditional update (<code>UPDATE ... SET qty = qty - 1 WHERE qty &gt;= 1</code>, then check rows affected) or a <code>CHECK (quantity &gt;= 0)</code> constraint so the database refuses to go negative.</li></ol></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Schema</div><div class="learn-code">product\n  id            BIGINT      PK\n  name          TEXT        NOT NULL\n  description   TEXT\n  brand_id      BIGINT      FK -> brand(id)\n  created_at    TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL\n\nproduct_variant                   -- what you actually buy and stock\n  id            BIGINT      PK\n  product_id    BIGINT      FK -> product(id) NOT NULL\n  sku           TEXT        UNIQUE NOT NULL\n  attributes    JSONB                        -- {"size":"M","colour":"blue"}\n  price         DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL       -- the CURRENT price\n\ninventory                         -- stock per variant per warehouse\n  variant_id    BIGINT      FK -> product_variant(id)\n  warehouse_id  BIGINT      FK -> warehouse(id)\n  quantity      INT         NOT NULL CHECK (quantity &gt;= 0)\n  PRIMARY KEY (variant_id, warehouse_id)\n\ncart          (id PK, customer_id FK, created_at, updated_at)\ncart_item     (cart_id FK, variant_id FK, quantity INT CHECK (quantity &gt; 0),\n               PRIMARY KEY (cart_id, variant_id))\n\n"order"                           -- quoted: ORDER is a reserved SQL word\n  id            BIGINT      PK\n  customer_id   BIGINT      FK -> customer(id) NOT NULL\n  status        ENUM(\'PENDING\',\'PAID\',\'SHIPPED\',\'DELIVERED\',\'CANCELLED\') NOT NULL\n  placed_at     TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL\n  subtotal      DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL\n  discount      DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL\n  shipping      DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL\n  total         DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL\n  idempotency_key TEXT      UNIQUE            -- stops double-submitted checkouts\n\norder_line\n  id            BIGINT      PK\n  order_id      BIGINT      FK -> "order"(id) NOT NULL\n  variant_id    BIGINT      FK -> product_variant(id) NOT NULL\n  quantity      INT         NOT NULL\n  unit_price    DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL   -- SNAPSHOT. never join to current price.\n  product_name  TEXT        NOT NULL     -- snapshot too: names change\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_order_customer_time ON "order"(customer_id, placed_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_variant_product      ON product_variant(product_id);</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Class design: promotions</div><p class="learn-p">Promotions are the varying part, so they get the pattern work. Two are in play, and knowing which to use where is the whole answer:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Strategy</b> for "how is this one discount computed" &mdash; percentage, fixed amount, buy-2-get-1.</li><li><b>Decorator</b>-style chaining for "apply several promotions in order", because promotions stack and the order matters.</li></ul><div class="learn-code">#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nstruct CartLine {\n    long        variantId;\n    int         quantity;\n    double      unitPrice;\n    std::string category;\n};\n\nstruct PriceBreakdown {\n    double subtotal = 0.0;\n    double discount = 0.0;\n    double shipping = 0.0;\n    double total() const { return subtotal - discount + shipping; }\n};\n\n// ---- Strategy: one promotion rule --------------------------------------\nclass Promotion {\npublic:\n    virtual ~Promotion() = default;\n    virtual std::string name() const = 0;\n    // returns the discount THIS promotion adds, given the cart and running totals\n    virtual double discountFor(const std::vector&lt;CartLine&gt;&amp; lines,\n                               const PriceBreakdown&amp; running) const = 0;\n};\n\nclass PercentOffCategory : public Promotion {\n    std::string category_;\n    double      percent_;\npublic:\n    PercentOffCategory(std::string c, double p)\n        : category_(std::move(c)), percent_(p) {}\n    std::string name() const override { return percent_ &lt; 1 ? "PERCENT_OFF" : "PERCENT_OFF"; }\n    double discountFor(const std::vector&lt;CartLine&gt;&amp; lines,\n                       const PriceBreakdown&amp;) const override {\n        double sum = 0.0;\n        for (const auto&amp; l : lines)\n            if (l.category == category_) sum += l.unitPrice * l.quantity;\n        return sum * percent_;\n    }\n};\n\nclass BuyTwoGetOneFree : public Promotion {\n    std::string category_;\npublic:\n    explicit BuyTwoGetOneFree(std::string c) : category_(std::move(c)) {}\n    std::string name() const override { return "B2G1"; }\n    double discountFor(const std::vector&lt;CartLine&gt;&amp; lines,\n                       const PriceBreakdown&amp;) const override {\n        double d = 0.0;\n        for (const auto&amp; l : lines)\n            if (l.category == category_)\n                d += (l.quantity / 3) * l.unitPrice;   // every 3rd item free\n        return d;\n    }\n};\n\nclass FreeShippingOver : public Promotion {\n    double threshold_;\npublic:\n    explicit FreeShippingOver(double t) : threshold_(t) {}\n    std::string name() const override { return "FREE_SHIPPING"; }\n    double discountFor(const std::vector&lt;CartLine&gt;&amp;,\n                       const PriceBreakdown&amp; running) const override {\n        return running.subtotal &gt;= threshold_ ? running.shipping : 0.0;\n    }\n};\n\n// ---- The engine: applies promotions in a defined order ------------------\nclass PricingEngine {\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Promotion&gt;&gt; promotions_;   // order is significant\npublic:\n    void add(std::unique_ptr&lt;Promotion&gt; p) { promotions_.push_back(std::move(p)); }\n\n    PriceBreakdown price(const std::vector&lt;CartLine&gt;&amp; lines, double baseShipping) const {\n        PriceBreakdown b;\n        for (const auto&amp; l : lines) b.subtotal += l.unitPrice * l.quantity;\n        b.shipping = baseShipping;\n\n        for (const auto&amp; p : promotions_)\n            b.discount += p-&gt;discountFor(lines, b);\n\n        if (b.discount &gt; b.subtotal + b.shipping)     // never pay the customer\n            b.discount = b.subtotal + b.shipping;\n        return b;\n    }\n};</div><div class="learn-tip"><b>The trade-off to raise unprompted:</b> "Promotions stacking means order matters &mdash; 10% off then &pound;5 off is not the same as &pound;5 off then 10% off. I\'ve made the order explicit in the list rather than leaving it emergent. A real system usually also needs rules about which promotions can combine at all, and I\'d model that as a priority plus an exclusivity group rather than hard-coding it."</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">API</div><div class="learn-code">GET   /products?q=shirt&amp;category=apparel&amp;limit=20&amp;cursor=...\nGET   /products/{id}                      -> includes its variants\nPOST  /carts/{cartId}/items               { "variantId":"v_1","quantity":2 }\n     200 -> the recalculated cart with the price breakdown\nDELETE /carts/{cartId}/items/{variantId}\nPOST  /orders                             Idempotency-Key: &lt;uuid&gt;\n      { "cartId":"c_9", "addressId":"ad_2", "paymentToken":"tok_x" }\n      201 { "orderId":"or_77", "total":"42.50", "status":"PENDING" }\n      409 { "error":{ "code":"OUT_OF_STOCK",\n                      "details":{ "variantId":"v_1","available":0 } } }</div><p class="learn-p">Say why checkout is the one endpoint that <i>must</i> carry an idempotency key: a customer who double-clicks, or a mobile client that retries on a flaky connection, must not be charged twice.</p></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Problem bank',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Overview</div><p class="learn-p">Six more, in the form you would get them. Try each yourself for ten minutes before reading the solution &mdash; reading solutions creates recognition, not recall, and the interview tests recall.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Q1. Design an elevator control system</div><p class="learn-p"><b>What it tests:</b> the State pattern, and whether you conflate a single car with the bank.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Solution sketch.</b> Two levels: <code>Elevator</code> (one car) and <code>ElevatorController</code> (dispatches across cars). Candidates who model only one car miss the interesting half.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>State</b> on the car: <code>Idle</code>, <code>MovingUp</code>, <code>MovingDown</code>, <code>DoorsOpen</code>, <code>Maintenance</code>. Each state answers "what happens on a request?" differently &mdash; a request while <code>DoorsOpen</code> queues, a request while <code>Maintenance</code> is rejected. That kills the big switch.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Strategy</b> for dispatch: nearest-car, or the classic <i>elevator algorithm</i> (keep going in one direction serving requests until none remain that way). Swapping the strategy changes the whole behaviour of the bank, which is exactly why it should be an object.</p><div class="learn-code">class Elevator;                   // forward declarations\nenum class Direction { Up, Down };\n\nclass ElevatorState {\npublic:\n    virtual ~ElevatorState() = default;\n    virtual void requestFloor(Elevator&amp; e, int floor) = 0;\n    virtual void tick(Elevator&amp; e) = 0;\n    virtual const char* name() const = 0;\n};\n\nclass DispatchStrategy {\npublic:\n    virtual ~DispatchStrategy() = default;\n    virtual int chooseCar(const std::vector&lt;Elevator&gt;&amp; cars, int floor, Direction d) const = 0;\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Schema:</b> mostly operational &mdash; <code>elevator(id, bank_id, current_floor, state)</code>, <code>trip_request(id, from_floor, to_floor, requested_at, served_by, served_at)</code>. Say the request log exists for analytics: average wait time is the business metric.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Q2. Design a movie ticket booking system</div><p class="learn-p"><b>What it tests:</b> concurrency on a shared scarce resource, and the abstract/physical split again.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The split:</b> <code>Movie</code> (the film) vs <code>Show</code> (a screening at a time in a screen) vs <code>Seat</code> (physical, belongs to a screen) vs <code>ShowSeat</code> (this seat, at this show &mdash; the thing that is actually bookable). Missing <code>ShowSeat</code> is the classic error: seat 14A is not booked, it is booked <i>for the 7pm showing</i>.</p><div class="learn-code">show_seat (show_id FK, seat_id FK, status ENUM(\'AVAILABLE\',\'HELD\',\'BOOKED\'),\n           held_until TIMESTAMPTZ NULL, booking_id FK NULL,\n           PRIMARY KEY (show_id, seat_id))</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The concurrency answer &mdash; the point of the question.</b> Seat selection needs a temporary <b>hold</b>, not an immediate booking, because the user needs a few minutes to pay. So: <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> on the chosen rows inside a transaction, set <code>HELD</code> with <code>held_until = now() + 5 minutes</code>, and have a sweeper (or a lazy check on read) release expired holds. Payment success converts hold to <code>BOOKED</code>. Mention that holds must expire, or a malicious user can freeze a whole cinema for free.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>API:</b> <code>POST /shows/{id}/holds</code> &rarr; 201 with an expiry; <code>POST /bookings</code> with the hold id and an idempotency key.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Q3. Design a notification service</div><p class="learn-p"><b>What it tests:</b> Observer plus Strategy, and whether you think about delivery guarantees.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Strategy</b> per channel (<code>EmailChannel</code>, <code>SmsChannel</code>, <code>PushChannel</code>) behind one <code>NotificationChannel</code> interface. <b>Observer</b> so domain events fan out to whoever cares. <b>Template Method</b> for the send pipeline: render, validate, throttle, dispatch, record &mdash; with only render and dispatch varying.</p><div class="learn-code">struct Recipient;                 // forward declarations\nstruct RenderedMessage;\n\nclass NotificationChannel {\npublic:\n    virtual ~NotificationChannel() = default;\n    virtual bool send(const Recipient&amp; to, const RenderedMessage&amp; msg) = 0;\n    virtual const char* channelName() const = 0;\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>Schema:</b> <code>notification(id, user_id, template_id, channel, status, attempts, created_at, sent_at, failed_reason)</code>, plus <code>user_preference(user_id, channel, category, enabled)</code> so users can mute categories. Add <code>template(id, channel, locale, body)</code> &mdash; and note the <code>locale</code>, because someone always forgets internationalisation.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>The senior point:</b> sending is unreliable, so it is a queue with retries and exponential backoff, an idempotency key per (event, user, channel) so a retried event does not double-send, and a dead-letter queue for permanent failures.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Q4. Design a file system</div><p class="learn-p"><b>What it tests:</b> Composite, plainly. Also whether you can handle recursion cleanly.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Composite</b> exactly as in <b>The rest of the pattern catalogue</b>: <code>FileNode</code> base, <code>File</code> leaf, <code>Directory</code> holding <code>vector&lt;unique_ptr&lt;FileNode&gt;&gt;</code>, with <code>size()</code> recursing. Add a <b>Visitor</b>-ish traversal if they push on "how would you search", or just an iterator.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Schema</b> is the interesting twist &mdash; trees in SQL. Offer the options and pick:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>Adjacency list</b> &mdash; <code>node(id, parent_id, name, type, size)</code>. Trivial to write, but "give me the full path" or "everything under this folder" needs a recursive query (<code>WITH RECURSIVE</code>).</li><li><b>Materialised path</b> &mdash; store <code>/a/b/c</code> as a column. Subtree queries become a simple <code>LIKE \'/a/b/%\'</code>. Renaming a folder means rewriting descendants.</li><li><b>Closure table</b> &mdash; a row per ancestor-descendant pair. Fast both directions, more storage and more write work.</li></ul><p class="learn-p">Naming all three and choosing based on read/write mix is a strong answer. Add <code>UNIQUE (parent_id, name)</code> &mdash; you cannot have two files with the same name in one directory.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Q5. Design a vending machine</div><p class="learn-p"><b>What it tests:</b> State, and careful handling of money.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>States:</b> <code>Idle</code>, <code>HasMoney</code>, <code>Dispensing</code>, <code>OutOfStock</code>. Every input (insert coin, select item, cancel) means something different per state, which is precisely the State pattern\'s case. Draw the transition table &mdash; drawing it is worth more than describing it.</p><div class="learn-code">class Machine;                    // forward declaration\n\nclass VendingState {\npublic:\n    virtual ~VendingState() = default;\n    virtual void insertCoin(Machine&amp;, int cents) = 0;\n    virtual void select(Machine&amp;, const std::string&amp; slot) = 0;\n    virtual void cancel(Machine&amp;) = 0;\n};</div><p class="learn-p"><b>The details that separate answers:</b> money in integer cents, never floating point; change calculation is a small coin-change problem and can <i>fail</i> (exact change only), which is a real state; and the dispense-then-decrement order matters if power can fail mid-transaction.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Q6. Design a ride-hailing service</div><p class="learn-p"><b>What it tests:</b> whether you keep this round\'s scope, since this is usually a System Design question.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>Keep it in scope.</b> Do not start with geo-sharding and Kafka. Model <code>Rider</code>, <code>Driver</code>, <code>Vehicle</code>, <code>Trip</code>, <code>TripStatus</code>, and the matching interface.</p><p class="learn-p"><b>State</b> on Trip: <code>Requested &rarr; Matched &rarr; DriverArrived &rarr; InProgress &rarr; Completed</code>, with <code>Cancelled</code> reachable from several &mdash; and note who is allowed to cancel changes by state, and whether a fee applies. <b>Strategy</b> for matching (nearest, highest-rated, best ETA) and for pricing (surge is a decorator over base fare).</p><div class="learn-code">trip (id PK, rider_id FK, driver_id FK NULL, status ENUM,\n      requested_at, matched_at, started_at, completed_at,\n      pickup_lat, pickup_lng, dropoff_lat, dropoff_lng,\n      fare DECIMAL(8,2) NULL, surge_multiplier DECIMAL(3,2))\ndriver_location (driver_id PK, lat, lng, updated_at)   -- hot table, written constantly</div><p class="learn-p">The one scaling sentence worth adding: "driver locations are written every few seconds and queried by proximity, so in production that is a geospatial index or a separate in-memory store rather than a normal table &mdash; but the relational model above is the logical view."</p></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Common mistakes and a self-check',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The ten ways people lose this round</div><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>#</th><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Designing before asking anything</td><td>Six questions, then read the scope back and get agreement</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Running out of time with one area untouched</td><td>Announce a time budget at the start and glance at the clock at the halfway mark</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Naming patterns to show off</td><td>Name a pattern only when you can state what breaks without it</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Drifting into System Design &mdash; caches, load balancers, sharding</td><td>Stay at classes, endpoints and tables. Mention scale once, at the end</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Missing the abstract-vs-physical split</td><td>Ask "is the thing they book the same as the thing in the catalogue?" Title/Copy, Product/Inventory, Movie/Show/Seat</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Joining to a live price for historical records</td><td>Snapshot price and name onto the order line</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>No mention of concurrency</td><td>Every scarce resource gets the transaction + constraint answer</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Silent designing</td><td>Narrate, and state the alternative you rejected</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Defensiveness when challenged</td><td>Their rubric explicitly values "responds to coaching". Treat a challenge as information</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>No trade-offs at the end</td><td>Reserve five minutes: what you\'d change at scale, what you left out, what you\'d revisit</td></tr></table></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Handling being challenged</div><p class="learn-p">They will push on something, and it is usually a test of how you think rather than a correction. The move is neither to cave instantly nor to dig in:</p><div class="learn-tip">"That\'s a fair point &mdash; if &lt;their scenario&gt; happens, my design does &lt;the bad thing&gt;. I chose it because &lt;original reason&gt;, but if &lt;their scenario&gt; is common then &lt;alternative&gt; is better because &lt;reason&gt;. Which is closer to how your traffic actually looks?"</div><p class="learn-p">That acknowledges, explains, adapts, and hands the decision back with a real question. It works whether they were right or just probing.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Self-check against the stated rubric</div><p class="learn-p">The brief names three things. Before you say you are done, verify:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li><b>API design</b> &mdash; did I give endpoints with methods, paths, a request body, a response body, at least one error case with a status code, and say something about pagination or idempotency?</li><li><b>Class design</b> &mdash; does each class do one thing? Did I use composition where the behaviour varies? Did I justify each pattern by what would break without it? Did I write <code>virtual ~Class()</code>?</li><li><b>Data schema</b> &mdash; did I name every primary key and foreign key, mark NOT NULL and UNIQUE, give at least one index with a reason, and handle the many-to-many with a join table?</li></ul></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The last five minutes &mdash; a script</div><p class="learn-p">Do not let the session peter out. Close deliberately:</p><div class="learn-tip">"To summarise: &lt;three sentences on the model&gt;. If this grew 100&times;, the first thing to break would be &lt;X&gt;, and I\'d fix it by &lt;Y&gt;. I deliberately left out &lt;A and B&gt;. The part I\'m least sure about is &lt;C&gt; &mdash; I\'d want to know more about &lt;real-world constraint&gt; before committing to it."</div><p class="learn-p">Naming the weakest part of your own design is counter-intuitive but reads as senior. It proves you evaluated it rather than just produced it.</p></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Questions to ask them</div><ol class="learn-list"><li>How much of your codebase is in this shape today, and where does it hurt?</li><li>Where do you draw the line between putting a rule in the database and putting it in the application?</li><li>How do schema migrations work here &mdash; what does changing a table on a live service look like?</li><li>When a design decision is contested on a team, how does it get resolved?</li></ol></div>',
    },
    {
      t: 'Cheat sheet',
      learn: '<div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The 45 minutes</div><div class="learn-code">0-6    requirements: 6 questions, write the scope, get agreement\n6-10   nouns -> entities -> cardinalities\n10-22  deep dive 1   (usually schema)\n22-34  deep dive 2   (usually API)\n34-40  third area, lighter\n40-45  trade-offs, what you left out, your questions</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The six opening questions</div><div class="learn-code">1. Who are the users, and what are their top 3 actions?\n2. What\'s explicitly out of scope?\n3. Roughly what scale?\n4. Read-heavy or write-heavy?\n5. What must be consistent, what can be stale?\n6. Anything unusual about this domain?</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Pattern triggers</div><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>You hear...</th><th>Reach for</th></tr><tr><td>"we might add other kinds of X later"</td><td>Strategy</td></tr><tr><td>"when this happens, several things must react"</td><td>Observer</td></tr><tr><td>"optional extras in any combination"</td><td>Decorator</td></tr><tr><td>"undo", "queue of work", "replay"</td><td>Command</td></tr><tr><td>a big switch on a status field</td><td>State</td></tr><tr><td>"same steps, one part differs"</td><td>Template Method</td></tr><tr><td>tree, nesting, part-whole</td><td>Composite</td></tr><tr><td>"this third-party library doesn\'t fit"</td><td>Adapter</td></tr><tr><td>"the client has to call six things in order"</td><td>Facade</td></tr><tr><td>"only create it if it\'s actually used"</td><td>Proxy (virtual)</td></tr><tr><td>families of things used together</td><td>Abstract Factory</td></tr></table></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The nine principles</div><div class="learn-code">1. Separate what varies from what stays the same\n2. Program to an interface, not an implementation\n3. Favor composition over inheritance\n4. Strive for loosely coupled designs\n5. Open for extension, closed for modification\n6. Depend on abstractions, not concrete classes\n7. Talk only to your immediate friends (Least Knowledge)\n8. Don\'t call us, we\'ll call you (Hollywood)\n9. One class, one reason to change</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">C++ reflexes</div><div class="learn-code">virtual ~Base() = default;        on every polymorphic base   <- do this every time\noverride                          on every override\nunique_ptr by default; shared_ptr deliberately; weak_ptr for observers\nconst T&amp; for read-only params\nenum class, not plain enum\nRule of Zero: let the compiler generate the special members\nstd::function instead of a hierarchy for a single stateless behaviour</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">Schema reflexes</div><div class="learn-code">surrogate PK; natural key as UNIQUE\nFK on the "many" side; join table for many-to-many (and it usually has its own columns)\nDECIMAL for money, never FLOAT\nTIMESTAMPTZ in UTC\nsnapshot price + name onto order lines\nindex every FK; composite index order = leftmost prefix rule\npartial UNIQUE index to make double-booking impossible\nCHECK constraints for invariants\ncreated_at / updated_at on anything humans edit</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">HTTP</div><div class="learn-code">200 ok   201 created   202 accepted   204 no content\n400 malformed   401 not authenticated   403 not allowed   404 missing\n409 CONFLICT (already booked / already exists)   422 semantically invalid\n429 rate limited   500 our fault\n\nPOST isn\'t idempotent -> Idempotency-Key header on create/pay\ncursor pagination for feeds, offset for admin page numbers\nidentity comes from the token, never from the request body</div></div><div class="learn-section"><div class="learn-h">The three sentences that earn the most</div><div class="learn-code">"A &lt;title&gt; isn\'t a &lt;copy&gt; - one is the catalogue entry, one is the\n physical thing you actually book."\n\n"The order line snapshots the price, so history doesn\'t change when\n the product\'s price does."\n\n"Two requests can both pass the availability check, so I\'d put a unique\n constraint on it and return 409 when the database refuses the second."</div></div>',
    },
  ],
};

// Slot it into System Design, immediately after Patterns & Case Studies.
if (typeof SYSDES_CONTENT !== 'undefined' && SYSDES_CONTENT.tabs) {
  const at = SYSDES_CONTENT.tabs.findIndex(t => t.id === 'patterns');
  SYSDES_CONTENT.tabs.splice(at < 0 ? SYSDES_CONTENT.tabs.length : at + 1, 0, APPDESIGN_TAB);
}
